Subject: GE Plus: the GoldenEye XBLA release in its Xbox 360 package form

gebean.c reads the GoldenEye XBLA release only as its loose files: a folder
holding files/new/char, or a .7z/.zip with entries under it. The same release
also comes as the console's own download - one STFS package, title 584108A9,
740 MB - usually inside a .7z that keeps the console's folders
(<top>/0000000000000000/584108A9/000D0000/<package>). Every file in the package
but default.xex is byte for byte the loose release's (all 1,802 compared by
md5), so this is a second way in, not a second release.

Accepted now, found by content in added-content/ (and the older xbla/ dirs):
the package itself (STFS magic and title id 584108A9 at 0x360, never its
name), a folder holding it (GEBEAN_SCAN_DEPTH 2 -> 4: the folder as it comes
out of its archive has the package four names down), and a .7z or .zip holding
it (an entry under 584108A9/000D0000/, or named as the package). A .rar is still
never looked into.

The unpack fills the same cache the archive form does (files/ at the top of
cache/xbla/goldeneye) with the same files (gebeanWantEntry), on the same worker
thread behind the same notice, so nothing after it changes:
- the bare package (or a folder's) is read where it lies with x360.c's reader;
- an archive's package cannot be: it is inside one solid LZMA2 block. Rather
  than spooling 740 MB to disk to read 400 MB of it, the block is decoded as a
  stream (archive.c's new archiveStreamEntry(), which holds the decoder's
  dictionary and a 1 MB piece, never the entry) and the package's own layout is
  followed as it goes by: its file table comes first, gives every wanted file's
  4 KB blocks by their offsets in the package, and each block is written where
  it belongs as it passes. Only a package whose wanted files are not stored in
  consecutive blocks (which needs its hash tables followed) is spooled whole
  into the cache first and read from there; this release's are all consecutive.
- free space is checked before anything is written (iOS: the volume's capacity
  for important use, app/ios/PDXbla.m; elsewhere statvfs): what the unpack
  writes plus 64 MB. Without it, nothing is written, a notice says how much is
  needed for five seconds, the game goes on in the N64 look, the cache gets a
  .needs-space note for the settings page, and the next start tries again.

The cache's marker now names what it was unpacked from (file name and size),
so a release replaced by another file is unpacked again; a marker written
before this patch is empty and still good for the archive form it can only
have come from. Every unpack starts by emptying the cache (all but the
Community Edition's folder), so nothing a killed unpack left half written is
ever taken for a finished one; the marker is written last. The Community
Edition's patch is not offered the package as an archive to take more files
from (gebeanTreeInfo() names no archive for it).

Nothing here draws anything differently: with the loose form, or with no
GoldenEye files, the game reads exactly what it read before.

